          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a disease of the whole joint, not just a joint that has worn out. The reference summary describes it as a disorder affecting the cartilage, the bone underneath, the ligaments, the joint lining and the surrounding muscles. Cartilage is progressively lost, and the bone beneath it remodels in response (condition-osteoarthritis). It most often affects the knees, hips, hands and spine.\n\nThe \"wear and tear\" picture is described as inaccurate because it is an active process. The reference summary says it involves inflammation and attempted repair, not simple mechanical erosion (condition-osteoarthritis). This matters in practice. Believing the joint is simply wearing out can lead people to rest it, but the guidance describes muscle weakness from reduced use as making the joint worse. That is why exercise is described as the first-line treatment, not an add-on (condition-osteoarthritis; guideline-osteoarthritis-management).\n\nI could not retrieve detailed information on the specific cartilage and subchondral bone mechanisms, or on the risk factors. My final search did not return results, so I cannot describe those parts from the sources I checked. A clinician or a dedicated reference on osteoarthritis would be the best place to ask about them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
